(525799, 51679, 10402, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is an attractive photo that conveys something of the holiday season with the evergreen twig, ice, and red background. There are some interesting shapes and textures that are pleasing to look at. The focus is a bit soft, which adds to the festive feeling. What it lacks is something to catch the viewer''s attention; a center of interest to draw the viewer into the image. This dooms it to mediocrity. It would work well as an element of something larger, such as a printed program for a Christmas concert. But as a stand-alone work a typical person would glance at it, think "pretty", and move on.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is an attractive photo that conveys something of the holiday season with the evergreen twig, ice, and red background. There are some interesting shapes and textures that are pleasing to look at. The focus is a bit soft, which adds to the festive feeling. What it lacks is something to catch the viewer''s attention; a center of interest to draw the viewer into the image. This dooms it to mediocrity. It would work well as an element of something larger, such as a printed program for a Christmas concert. But as a stand-alone work a typical person would glance at it, think "pretty", and move on.', '2003-12-29 20:43:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(525827, 51769, 12733, '<td valign="top">I had quite a few requests for the recipe, so here goes:<br/>Mexican Wedding Cakes: 1/2 cup powdered sugar, 1 cup butter, softened, 2 teaspoons vanilla, 2 cups all-pupose flour, 1 cup finely chopped almonds or pecans. 1/4 teaspoon salt, additional powdered sugar for rolling. Preheat oven to 325. In a large bowl combine 1/2 cup powdered sugar, butter and vanilla. Blend well. Stir in flour, nuts and salt until dough holds together. Shape into 1/2-inch balls  about 1 tablespoon of dough. Do not flatten, these should be globes, with only the bottom a bit flattened. Place inch apart on ungreased cookie sheets. Bake for 15-20 minutes until set but not brown. Remove from cookie sheets. Cool slightly and roll in powdered sugar while still warm. Cool completely and reroll in powdered sugar.</td>', 'I had quite a few requests for the recipe, so here goes:Mexican Wedding Cakes: 1/2 cup powdered sugar, 1 cup butter, softened, 2 teaspoons vanilla, 2 cups all-pupose flour, 1 cup finely chopped almonds or pecans. 1/4 teaspoon salt, additional powdered sugar for rolling. Preheat oven to 325. In a large bowl combine 1/2 cup powdered sugar, butter and vanilla. Blend well. Stir in flour, nuts and salt until dough holds together. Shape into 1/2-inch balls  about 1 tablespoon of dough. Do not flatten, these should be globes, with only the bottom a bit flattened. Place inch apart on ungreased cookie sheets. Bake for 15-20 minutes until set but not brown. Remove from cookie sheets. Cool slightly and roll in powdered sugar while still warm. Cool completely and reroll in powdered sugar.', '2003-12-29 21:18:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(525859, 51662, 5351, '<td valign="top">Absolutely stunning! Very eye-catching and a well deserved win - thanks for sharing how it was done.</td>', 'Absolutely stunning! Very eye-catching and a well deserved win - thanks for sharing how it was done.', '2003-12-29 21:56:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(525878, 51662, 2634, '<td valign="top">wow, I had not voted on this during the chalenge, but seeing it now its fantastic!<br/>Congrats on a well done image.</td>', 'wow, I had not voted on this during the chalenge, but seeing it now its fantastic!Congrats on a well done image.', '2003-12-29 22:18:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(526167, 51799, 472, '<td valign="top">you know what? this is truly a stunning shot. you did great.  congrats !</td>', 'you know what? this is truly a stunning shot. you did great.  congrats !', '2003-12-30 10:17:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(526226, 51631, 9411, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique club</b> <br/> <br/>Very funny and original shot.  After reading your descripition I applaud you for coming up with it.<br/> <br/>I think what hurt this image was a lack of light. Hence the noise.<br/> <br/>What I''ve found when working with bad light is to try and compensate by reflecting as much of it as possible to increase what you have.<br/> <br/>A few pieces of white paper set around the popcorn could have added enough to brighten the image where you wouldn''t have had the noise problem.  I''m assuming you were working with a hotel lamp.<br/> <br/>Possibly you could have tried to not zoom as much as you did and given the illusion of a macro by cropping,, but I''m not 100% sure on that.<br/> <br/>It really is a good shot and the humour factor is a plus.<br/> <br/>JC</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique club  Very funny and original shot.  After reading your descripition I applaud you for coming up with it. I think what hurt this image was a lack of light. Hence the noise. What I''ve found when working with bad light is to try and compensate by reflecting as much of it as possible to increase what you have. A few pieces of white paper set around the popcorn could have added enough to brighten the image where you wouldn''t have had the noise problem.  I''m assuming you were working with a hotel lamp. Possibly you could have tried to not zoom as much as you did and given the illusion of a macro by cropping,, but I''m not 100% sure on that. It really is a good shot and the humour factor is a plus. JC', '2003-12-30 11:16:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(526307, 51148, 3569, '<td valign="top">Critique Club review:<br/> <br/>Hi JC,<br/> <br/>First, your shot fits the challenge well.  <br/> <br/>The photo is cropped nicely and the shapes of the leaf, feather and butterfly, I assume Black Swallowtail, wing fit the frame well.  The colors appear to be true to what I know about the three subjects. DOF and sharpness are not a problem and are good.  <br/> <br/>The only problems I see, is the black background only modifies the feather and leaf.  The veins of the wing, because they are also dark, are really lost.  Not sure if a different color would have helped or arranging the three objects differently.  Even though I like the three subjects, the feather, Blue Jay I assume, by itself would have really been nice.  The soft blue colors, with the nice down, and the dark background would have made an excellent macro subject.  You might try it if you didn''t.  <br/> <br/>As a whole I liked your shot, but I think you probably see some of the negatives and how to improve a great idea.  Good luck in the future.<br/> <br/>Have a Fantastic 2004,<br/> <br/>Van<br/></td>', 'Critique Club review: Hi JC, First, your shot fits the challenge well.   The photo is cropped nicely and the shapes of the leaf, feather and butterfly, I assume Black Swallowtail, wing fit the frame well.  The colors appear to be true to what I know about the three subjects. DOF and sharpness are not a problem and are good.   The only problems I see, is the black background only modifies the feather and leaf.  The veins of the wing, because they are also dark, are really lost.  Not sure if a different color would have helped or arranging the three objects differently.  Even though I like the three subjects, the feather, Blue Jay I assume, by itself would have really been nice.  The soft blue colors, with the nice down, and the dark background would have made an excellent macro subject.  You might try it if you didn''t.   As a whole I liked your shot, but I think you probably see some of the negatives and how to improve a great idea.  Good luck in the future. Have a Fantastic 2004, Van', '2003-12-30 13:19:35.000000', NULL, 0),
